    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Done. The build is green and the test suite passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nI made `householdId` deterministic and reworked household-based duplicate detection in `OwnerRestControllerV1.java` (with matching OpenAPI doc updates).\n\n**What changed:**\n\n1. **`householdId` is now always computed deterministically** \u2014 the first 12 hex chars of SHA-256 over `normalizedLastName + '|' + postcode` (was previously derived from last name + *address*, and only set when the caller opted into `sharesHousehold`). It's `null` only when no postcode is present. So any two owners with the same last name and postcode automatically share a `householdId`, with no back-fill step.\n\n2. **`sharesHousehold` now only bypasses the duplicate block** rather than creating the link. The old `backfillHousehold`/`householdAddressKey` helpers are gone since the shared id emerges from the hash itself.\n\n3. **New household-duplicate rule (`requireNoHouseholdDuplicate`)** \u2014 because the household is keyed on `(lastName, postcode)`, a second owner landing in an existing household (same computed `householdId`) is rejected as a household duplicate with **409** \u2014 unless the request sets `sharesHousehold`, in which case it's created as a declared member.\n\n4. **`markPossibleDuplicate` now keys off the computed `householdId`** and, per the spec, a declared household member (`sharesHousehold=true`) is **never** flagged as a possible duplicate.\n\nThe exact-identity check (`requireUniqueIdentity`, telephone|email|householdId) still runs first and is unaffected by `sharesHousehold`, so an exact duplicate is still a 409.\n\nNote: the task text mentioned \"the household-size input to the membership level,\" but this codebase's `toMembershipLevel` derives the level only from email presence and `namesakeCount` \u2014 there is no household-size input, so there was nothing to rewire there.\n\nThe one existing/new test (`coreSameLastNameAndPostcodeShareHouseholdId`) and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
